1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:10,960
If I'm bringing someone to solve a problem that I need someone to do to do QA on this model's work, and in four months, the next generation of the model could be, you know, I'm not gonna say ten x, five x better.

2
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:12,800
What do I do to that person then?

3
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:16,160
Is it right to hire someone in if I know that in four months, I'm gonna let them go?

4
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:22,640
Certainly on the development side, it's hiring someone that conducts like an orchestra rather than someone that writes code.

5
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:27,920
Kevin Scott likes to say, we've made a lot of difficult things trivial.

6
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,560
What impossible things have we made merely difficult?

7
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:32,240
What is the last human role?

8
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:47,360
Okay, welcome to the Catholic Futurist podcast here at the Ascent Summit in the FIO podcast booth.

9
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,240
My name is Louis Kim and I'm with Matthew Sanders, Taylor Black, Vincent Higgins.

10
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:59,040
Why don't we just start off with a few questions that I had that we can also share later in the panel.

11
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:08,240
What's one thing that has surprised you about AI adoption, either more quickly or more slowly than you've anticipated?

12
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:10,080
Matthew, may I start?

13
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:16,240
Well, I mean, adoption, I think at enterprise level, has not surprised me.

14
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,320
I think it's getting pretty intense, it's ramping up.

15
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:27,440
Obviously, you know, if there are ocean liners, they take a little bit of time to move, you know, in a very kind of controlled way, but I think they're all starting to see it's existential.

16
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:40,640
I am a little bit surprised how effective some, some of the propaganda has been to basically freak average people out that AI somehow is a risk to their future and therefore needs to be resisted.

17
00:01:40,960 --> 00:01:48,320
So I as a way of trying to steer humanity in a direction, I'm just going to reject AI or I'm just going to refuse to use it.

18
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:59,600
I just recently had a statistic, I mean, these statistics seem to come out, they seem to change every week to week, but only twenty percent of Americans are optimistic about the trajectory of AI.

19
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:02,160
I just think that's remarkable, for me.

20
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:16,320
And I feel somewhat convicted because those of us who work in the industry, we've done a really bad job, I think, of sharing how much the technology can be massive unlocking and overcoming some of the, you know, the biggest bans of human existence, disease, ignorance.

21
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,800
And yet, people are afraid of it.

22
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:25,120
So I think we have to do something about the adoption, particularly in the West, because China is going to continue to adopt.

23
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:36,000
And if we are concerned about a world in which China dominates AI, if we're refusing to use it because we're afraid of what it's going to do to our future, that's going to become an existential problem for the West.

24
00:02:36,880 --> 00:02:43,360
Taylor, you have a foot in sort of multiple worlds, in a big tech company as well as in the Catholic world.

25
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:53,680
How do you how do you see some of the either surprises and acceleration or slowdown or even kind of a of comment on this change of opinion that we've seen?

26
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:55,600
Yeah, I think there's two things.

27
00:02:55,840 --> 00:02:58,640
One is it's a hard technology to adopt well.

28
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:10,480
I think all of us use it for something like a Mad Lib for your brother's wedding or, you know, I used it yesterday for Saint John Chrysostom holding a stein of beer for our parish potluck or whatever, right?

29
00:03:10,960 --> 00:03:27,360
And those are simple and easy, but the hard are actually integrating them into a workflow, your own personal workflow or ways in which that still allows you to relate to other people well and not load cognitive capacity that you'll want later.

30
00:03:29,040 --> 00:03:30,320
It ends up being more nuanced.

31
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:39,760
And I think that lends itself to really chunky adoption, not only on personal levels, but also on SMB sorts of levels and inside enterprises.

32
00:03:41,200 --> 00:03:48,960
Enterprises, I think, have the benefit of all of their data and all of their processes and operations are already pretty locked down.

33
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:56,240
And so being able to use AI at all means that you're already using it in a fairly compliant sort of environment.

34
00:03:57,040 --> 00:04:01,680
But that's just not the case for a whole, a whole lot of small to medium sized businesses.

35
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:04,560
So that's been interesting.

36
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:14,000
The other interesting thing that I've been seeing as far as adoption goes is how it brings people's presuppositions about what it means to be human to the forefront.

37
00:04:14,240 --> 00:04:16,400
I mean, we talk about, you know, is AI human?

38
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:17,360
Is it not?

39
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:22,640
Like, I feel like that conversation has settled, thank God, because they're so different.

40
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:31,840
But, but if you have a relatively thin anthropology yourself, indeed, AI can do all the things that a human can do.

41
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:34,720
if you think that's what humans do.

42
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:39,760
So I think it's opened up a lot of really interesting conversations that wouldn't have happened otherwise.

43
00:04:40,800 --> 00:04:44,480
And that's always exciting from a novel technology standpoint.

44
00:04:45,280 --> 00:04:56,960
The fact that you bring up the human dependencies and how this AI era has actually ironically or paradoxically brought more focus to the human role, kind of leads to a segue.

45
00:04:57,280 --> 00:05:00,720
I think, Vincent, I want to post to you because you know, you run an industrial company.

46
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:03,040
It's not a pure AI firm, but you're using AI.

47
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:06,960
But I want to reference real quickly a conversation I had with a man who's a good friend.

48
00:05:06,960 --> 00:05:09,200
a conversation I had with some partners at Bain yesterday.

49
00:05:09,680 --> 00:05:13,200
And I asked them, I posed the same question to them.

50
00:05:13,760 --> 00:05:25,840
And they said, the adoption speed has been slower because people forget how much human dependence there is when they move away from the screen, the keyboard and the mouse.

51
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:30,080
There's what they call process debt built up in organizations.

52
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:34,800
And it's like ambulances running to a city.

53
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:37,040
You make them Ferrari's.

54
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:40,640
They're not going to go through lights and around corners any faster.

55
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:44,400
So do you want to comment on that as kind of a CEO of an industrial company?

56
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:58,480
Yeah, our AI really brings the power of that technology to the industrial field worker with a mobile device in their hand where they can talk to AI, get the answers they need to repair the pump or to understand the safety procedure.

57
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:03,520
But if AI answers the wrong way, there could be dire consequences.

58
00:06:03,760 --> 00:06:16,240
So adoption is perhaps slower than expected and they're watching every question and answer at the start, but starting to build confidence as they see more efficiencies in the field.

59
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:19,200
And it's never going to replace the wrench turner.

60
00:06:19,760 --> 00:06:27,520
Rather, it will support and improve safety, increase better operations and reliability for the workplace.

61
00:06:27,760 --> 00:06:43,360
So it's just taking longer than usual because all eyes are on how AI responds and how it's adopted in an engineer's approach to wanting to have control over the results.

62
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:49,280
One thing I heard from again this Bane partner is because Gen AI is not deterministic.

63
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:59,520
Even the AI engineers don't really understand what's going on inside the box that any labor savings that AI has created has been somewhat offset by QA and QC.

64
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:01,760
You still need a human in the loop.

65
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:15,600
So another question is how has your hiring profile changed for, let's say, the CIO IT manager type?

66
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:21,360
What's changed in terms of what you're looking for in this world of AI?

67
00:07:21,680 --> 00:07:22,480
Matthew, do you want to?

68
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:23,040
Sure.

69
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:26,560
I mean, to your comment about QA 2, I mean, I certainly, that is the case.

70
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:29,600
But it's, man, it's changing so fast.

71
00:07:30,640 --> 00:07:45,920
You know, back in November when Opus 4.5 came out, that was like, I think the Ion Robotics Age, that was the date of the Ion Robotics Age because effectively, in order to work at Anthropic, you have to pass a two hour test.

72
00:07:46,160 --> 00:07:47,120
It's extremely difficult.

73
00:07:47,520 --> 00:07:54,480
And that Opus 4.5 was the first model to do better than any human had in the history of Anthropic.

74
00:07:56,080 --> 00:08:05,360
So now this is a very specific domain, so we expect the models to do well considering how much time and energy goes into making sure they become masterful programmers, but we are starting to see them generalize.

75
00:08:05,440 --> 00:08:09,920
I mean, look, Astra is disconcertingly general.

76
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:23,840
I wouldn't call it AGI like Jensen did, but what I have been noticing because I work with agents every day from all the major labs is that every new model release, they do become a little bit better at something.

77
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:27,840
It's not to say that they're all equally capable in different areas, you have to kind of sus sus this.

78
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:33,600
And I can see, and this does affect hiring decisions.

79
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:45,920
If I'm bringing someone in to solve a problem that I need someone to do to do QA on this model's work, and in four months, the next generation of the model could be, you know, I'm not going to say ten x, five x better.

80
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:47,760
What do I do to that person then?

81
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:51,200
Is it right to hire someone in if I know that in four months I'm going to be letting them go?

82
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:57,280
Maybe I should just suck it up and just, you know, just try and grind through it because I just want to do that to a human person.

83
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:01,360
So it's just weird day to day to have to kind of wrestle through these things.

84
00:09:01,520 --> 00:09:04,640
I mean, we're hearing right now that, you know, effective.

85
00:09:04,640 --> 00:09:07,920
Actually, AI is not heavily disrupting the labour market right now.

86
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:09,360
There's more hiring going on.

87
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:16,960
But if you look, if you look closely at those statistics, it's the blue collar that's getting hired in to basically build AI infrastructure, right?

88
00:09:17,120 --> 00:09:19,680
Whereas the blue collar hiring is kind of frozen, at least.

89
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:25,760
So I think if you play this out, yes, we're going to see a bunch of blue collar workers get employed.

90
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:30,720
But if you follow this trend line forward, the AIs will continue to get masterful in white collar work.

91
00:09:31,120 --> 00:09:43,360
And the minute we have those embodied AIs and those robots become more and more capable, you don't think you're going to someone today who is thinking about hiring, you have to take this into consideration.

92
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:48,080
And I do think this is going to become increasingly a problem for the US administration.

93
00:09:48,960 --> 00:09:50,720
How are they going to assure people that they're going to have jobs?

94
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:55,680
And if they're not going to have jobs, how are they going to assure them that they're not going to lose their homes given the trend lines?

95
00:09:55,920 --> 00:10:07,520
And I should note as well, and then I'll shut up, I am worried how little conversation is happening outside of like the heads of a couple of labs about directionally where this technology is headed and what are we going to do about it.

96
00:10:08,240 --> 00:10:11,040
There's very little policy conversation, very little coming from politicians.

97
00:10:12,480 --> 00:10:19,440
Because people are aware that this is likely to have negative impact, generally what ends up happening is fear takes over and anger.

98
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:27,760
And so instead of talking about solutions, how are we going to get how are we going to move through this disruptive time together, we just get afraid and angry.

99
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:37,920
And then I'm afraid that that eventually will just build up and there will be this lashing out, which could be avoided if we simply were able to talk through it and figure out how we're going to get through this as a nation.

100
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:44,320
I know we've talked about, from a Catholic perspective, subsidiarity and sovereign AI.

101
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,720
So I want to get back to that.

102
00:10:46,720 --> 00:10:48,960
I'm sure you started to kind of go there.

103
00:10:49,680 --> 00:10:52,720
But I wanted to hear from Vincent on this question about a hiring profile.

104
00:10:54,880 --> 00:10:57,280
How has that changed in terms of what you're hiring for?

105
00:10:57,680 --> 00:11:05,360
Certainly on the development side, it's hiring someone that conducts like an orchestra rather than someone that writes code, that's a given.

106
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:12,000
And that person has to be more of a generalist and that person has to be very smart to use the tools well.

107
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:18,000
Everyone has the tools available at their disposal, but not everyone uses them effectively.

108
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:24,800
head of AI is a 40-year veteran of the industry in software development.

109
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:32,160
And he just orchestrates, he has agents running with a small team that does the work of a big team.

110
00:11:35,600 --> 00:11:44,560
As we think about, Taylor, you talked about, you know, AI is not human and it's a bit of a tired topic, you know, especially at kind of conferences.

111
00:11:45,920 --> 00:11:52,240
I think for me, it's challenge how I think about human-human relationships.

112
00:11:52,640 --> 00:11:59,840
You know, if we're so critical that AI is sort of affecting and compromising machine to people relationships.

113
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:04,640
You know, my thought is, well, you know, people to people relationships have some areas of improvement.

114
00:12:05,200 --> 00:12:17,200
I think where this might become acute is layoffs, firings, AI is going to disrupt obviously your staff makeup, and you're going to have to make hard decisions.

115
00:12:18,880 --> 00:12:24,480
How, you know, how pro, I think all of us have had experience letting people go.

116
00:12:26,880 --> 00:12:28,560
How proud do you feel like that all the way that.

117
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:31,760
that the way that has gone has aligned with Catholic social teaching.

118
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:33,280
Maybe you start.

119
00:12:34,240 --> 00:12:35,360
Yeah, no, it's a good question.

120
00:12:36,560 --> 00:12:41,760
I think there are a couple of things that are helpful for framing and for the transition period.

121
00:12:43,520 --> 00:12:47,680
Of course, without cause, right, lay off, all sorts of modalities.

122
00:12:49,680 --> 00:13:02,640
First off is helping yourself realize that you're doing the human a disservice if you're keeping them in a position that is not going to have marketability on the market.

123
00:13:03,360 --> 00:13:08,560
In the end, right, you're grateful for that person being a part of your company, but they have their own career to think about.

124
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:25,040
And ensuring that they are able to maintain their market value as a worker, as a laborer, ideally in something they enjoy, but also ensuring that they're able to keep bread on the table, I think is an important task that every employer has.

125
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:29,840
And so that can be done in a variety of different ways in a layoff sort of context.

126
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:47,280
If you're lucky enough to have the resources as a good employer, you know, being able to do a retraining, being able to do a kind of a refocus of where their skill set can be in anticipation of where new work is always, of course, is a great way of going about it.

127
00:13:47,840 --> 00:14:00,560
There's also, I think, a thing that Matthew alluded to this a little bit from the market economy standpoint, but there's a thing that's important, I think, for firms to understand too, is that AI isn't fantastic just for efficiencies.

128
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:07,280
It's also fantastic at approaching new kinds of problems that previously weren't economically possible.

129
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:16,640
And so this is where I encourage business owners and those who are employers to really open their innovation lid a little bit.

130
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:28,000
Like what were the things that we put on the shelf because they didn't work, but now we might have an efficiency that makes that a great business line for us or a new kind of customer problem that we're able to solve.

131
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:31,440
Those kinds of advances are much harder, right?

132
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:33,360
We're not dealing with the status quo.

133
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,600
We're not dealing with the way in which things always have been.

134
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:41,520
Kevin Scott likes to say, we've made a lot of difficult things trivial.

135
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,160
What impossible things have we made merely difficult?

136
00:14:46,640 --> 00:14:57,120
I want to go back now to the subsidiary sovereign AI topic that you've brought up in other conversations, but I also want to give a heads up.

137
00:14:57,360 --> 00:15:05,280
I'm going to ask each one of you to ask one of us a question so you can kind of use this time and Matthew, I might warn you in advance.

138
00:15:05,360 --> 00:15:06,800
I know you get on a little bit of a soapbox.

139
00:15:06,960 --> 00:15:10,000
I might have to tap you to move on.

140
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:18,480
But as AI, obviously, you know, so centralized with these big frontier companies, because all the capital necessary.

141
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:20,960
What's a Catholic view on that?

142
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:27,040
Well, I mean, the kind of Pope gave pretty clear direction on this, right?

143
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:36,400
I mean, he said this, the centralization that's occurring right now is an existential, I think, threat to freedom, but I think also to agency.

144
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:40,320
And I think this is something that we all have to internalize.

145
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:42,640
The Pope went as far as I think he possibly can.

146
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:43,920
I mean, he was pretty explicit.

147
00:15:44,400 --> 00:15:46,720
The centralization will lead to concentration of power, right?

148
00:15:46,960 --> 00:15:50,400
And then we'll have a few people making profound decisions which affect all of humanity.

149
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:51,520
This is not good, right?

150
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:52,240
This is not good.

151
00:15:52,720 --> 00:15:53,600
History has taught us this.

152
00:15:53,680 --> 00:15:54,800
And so what do we do about it?

153
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:57,040
What we can't do about it is just kind of drift, right?

154
00:15:57,280 --> 00:16:02,320
It's just let these forces feel they're inevitable and let these things just happen.

155
00:16:02,480 --> 00:16:07,840
I I don't even think the people who are behind AI, the guys who are heads of the labs, even want this.

156
00:16:08,320 --> 00:16:13,600
I think what's happened is they've had to raise so much money to move their vision forward.

157
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:18,240
They've become beholden to economic interests, even though personally they feel maybe very convicted.

158
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:20,480
They're looking to be liberated from their shareholders.

159
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:21,200
I really believe this.

160
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:24,720
We've had Demise Haspers and others say, we have to regulate this.

161
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:29,760
I guarantee you, the shareholders are not sitting around saying saying, That's what we want you to say is to advocate for regulation.

162
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,440
No, this is something that they personally believe is necessary because they have children.

163
00:16:33,680 --> 00:16:35,200
They care about the future they grow up in.

164
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:48,880
One thing I feel very convinced about is we, as we have to first of all get informed, but then we have to look for practical ways to avoid this drifting, or I should say, this gravitational pull towards centralization.

165
00:16:49,040 --> 00:16:50,240
I think there are some things we can do.

166
00:16:51,040 --> 00:16:55,920
One, we have to enable and support sovereign AI and sovereign AI at the household level.

167
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:59,040
Now, everyone likes to say that, but how do we do that?

168
00:16:59,200 --> 00:16:59,840
I think there are ways.

169
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:03,280
I mean, one is we know right now that there's no compute, right?

170
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:05,440
And all the labs are buying it up years in advance.

171
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:12,720
Well, if we're really going to get behind the vision of sovereign AI, the fact that labs are buying it up years in advance is not going to help the average consumer of the house sold.

172
00:17:12,960 --> 00:17:16,800
So how do we ensure that there is compute available that they can buy at a price point they can afford?

173
00:17:17,440 --> 00:17:20,800
Well, we have the technology, the DJX Sparks NVIDIA is out there.

174
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:22,720
It was supposed to be, I think, $3,500.

175
00:17:23,280 --> 00:17:26,160
Now it's up to $7, and it's going up and up.

176
00:17:26,320 --> 00:17:31,120
So one thing I think we need to do is we need to make sure that our elected officials know that we care about running our own AI.

177
00:17:31,280 --> 00:17:36,880
We don't want to have to have every question we ask have to go to a lab, and especially in those sensitive issues.

178
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:44,960
We should have the right to have compute and run models at home, and we should be able to have the power to power those models at home.

179
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:48,000
We shouldn't have to, every question shouldn't have to go to a major lab.

180
00:17:48,480 --> 00:17:53,760
And I think this is a vision of AI that is very much in line with the principal subsidiary and the church.

181
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:56,080
This is a way to resist centralization.

182
00:17:56,480 --> 00:18:04,800
And it's important to remember that the average small model today in the right harness can take care of ninety to ninety five percent of your questions and jobs you need to do.

183
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:10,240
So if that's the case, then why would I be paying a lab when I can basically pay for the cost of electricity at home.

184
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:14,880
So if this is something that makes sense, then I think we have to tell our politicians we want something done.

185
00:18:15,040 --> 00:18:17,760
And there's something, you know, I think very straightforward they could do just to be practical.

186
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:25,760
I think they could go to someone like Jensen Wong at Navidea and say, instead of selling all your computers to the major labs, we want you to sell twenty percent to the consumer.

187
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,240
You can still make a profit from that.

188
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:31,520
But we think this is important for our citizens to be able to leverage the technology.

189
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:37,920
And I think Jensen Wong having just bought Hugging Face and investing in Pool City, I think he would be very much on board with that vision.

190
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:40,000
I think several of the heads of the labs would be as well.

191
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:46,960
But their shareholders are not, he's not going to be able to go to shareholders and say, hey, what's up not going to be able to go to shareholders and say, I want to sell twenty percent of our computers to the average individual, to millions of people.

192
00:18:47,440 --> 00:18:50,080
Do you not trust the centralization of power?

193
00:18:50,560 --> 00:18:51,200
No, not at all.

194
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:55,520
It's deeply embedded in Catholic intellectual tradition, right?

195
00:18:56,400 --> 00:19:02,480
And that's us for, and not everyone may be familiar with the nuances of subsidiarity, but do you want to expand a little bit on?

196
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:09,200
Yeah, I mean, effectively the idea behind subsidiarity is if something can be done at a lower level, it should be.

197
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:13,600
Because effectively the decision making should be as close to the metal, as close to the people as possible.

198
00:19:13,840 --> 00:19:17,680
So the more you abstract away from the people, the more likely things are to break down, right?

199
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:19,520
And I think we all instinctively know this.

200
00:19:19,600 --> 00:19:21,760
This is one of the reasons why we don't trust our politicians.

201
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:23,040
Well, there's human sin.

202
00:19:23,200 --> 00:19:32,080
And, you know, the founding fathers built the checks and balances based on, you know, going back to Greek philosophy of Polybius, you know, transparency and the distribution of power.

203
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:32,640
Yeah.

204
00:19:33,120 --> 00:19:35,840
And so, listen, China's got one approach to this, and, right?

205
00:19:36,080 --> 00:19:44,800
And I think, you know, if we drift towards centralization, we're going to have a few people who have enormous influence over our political system by virtue of the amount of power and wealth they have.

206
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:51,360
I mean, going back to Taylor's point, I mean, China's solution to the employment crisis is, in fact, telling the companies, you can't fire them anyone.

207
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:52,720
That's one way to go.

208
00:19:53,040 --> 00:19:59,280
But be, you know, the interesting thing about that is I think forcing someone to remain in a job, which they probably don't even like to begin with, is not a solution.

209
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:00,800
You know, that's not moving forward.

210
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:13,920
I think the challenge is going to be recognizing that people all, everyone should be able to leverage AI to hopefully make their dreams come true, like so they can find a way to discern who they are and realize that their purpose.

211
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:18,800
And I think if freeing people from drudgery so they can do that, it's not a bad thing.

212
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:22,960
Making sure that the current economic system doesn't destroy them in the process, I think has to be the goal.

213
00:20:23,360 --> 00:20:23,360
Okay.

214
00:20:24,240 --> 00:20:29,120
We're going to close with each of you is gets to ask a question to one of us.

215
00:20:29,840 --> 00:20:31,280
I will ask a question to Taylor.

216
00:20:31,680 --> 00:20:43,760
And it follows on what Matthew was asking or speaking about related to having more freedom, more free time, more leisure time, and what do we do with that with our families?

217
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:46,080
Or would the opposite happen?

218
00:20:46,400 --> 00:20:52,640
We'll just get more work piled on our plate because there's more optimization of our time.

219
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:56,000
Yeah, I think that has to do with the nature of the firm.

220
00:20:56,560 --> 00:21:06,240
And that's why I think conferences like Center are so fantastic because they talk about building the firm in such a way that it has a Catholic character.

221
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:11,920
I was recently on a forum where we were battling about what it meant to be a Catholic firm.

222
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:16,400
And of course, in the end, in a strict definition, the only thing that can be Catholic is a human.

223
00:21:17,920 --> 00:21:26,240
But taking that concept of a Catholic firm and kind of pulling it apart, is it working forty hours a week?

224
00:21:26,640 --> 00:21:28,800
Why did we arbitrarily choose forty hours a week?

225
00:21:32,160 --> 00:21:37,680
who's the head of the firm has a more valuable job than a stay at home parent.

226
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:41,280
Well, I'm not sure that's the case either in the Catholic worldview.

227
00:21:41,440 --> 00:22:04,960
And so I think there's a bunch of opportunity that this technology disruption has given us as Catholic builders and in rearchitecting the firm and reunderstanding the nature of work, such that we put our value propositions where our theology and philosophy are and precisely what that looks like.

228
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:39,680
I think there's been some really interesting experiments on what that looks like, you know, a $5,000 bonus for getting married or limiting having all solemn holy days off or, you know, different things like that that still work very much for the economics of the firm, but start to bring in this more Catholic character that doesn't just take the status quo as it has developed intelligently but randomly historically into what we have now through a bunch of different competing interests.

229
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:53,360
I'm astonished that there isn't more conversation about the possibilities of artificial intelligence for research and the built physical world.

230
00:22:54,720 --> 00:23:02,240
Based on where you are and how you're seen from an industrial standpoint, can you kind of crack that open a little bit, shed a little bit of light on what you're seeing there?

231
00:23:02,960 --> 00:23:09,120
Yeah, so physical AI is basically AI that reaches us in the real world, not just on our computer screens.

232
00:23:10,720 --> 00:23:14,640
In my business, we build edge computing hardware where the model stays local.

233
00:23:15,520 --> 00:23:18,080
and some of the decisions are made locally on the model.

234
00:23:18,400 --> 00:23:23,280
So if you do get disconnected from the internet, you still get your AI available to you.

235
00:23:23,440 --> 00:23:28,400
And that's very important for field work because a lot of times you do lose that connectivity.

236
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:38,720
We're seeing on the physical AI side microphones and cameras being developed where it's capturing more of your world kind of in a scary way.

237
00:23:40,160 --> 00:23:46,000
I've seen some designs to wear these earbuds with cameras on them that have they understand your context.

238
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:48,960
So when you're walking into the grocery store, it's a little bit more.

239
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:53,040
or it understands what things you're choosing, what shelf you're on.

240
00:23:53,760 --> 00:23:57,840
And the privacy question then comes out with that as well.

241
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:04,800
So it's a bit of a slippery slope when physical AI touches every moment of your day.

242
00:24:05,440 --> 00:24:11,760
I think we get to be very cognizant that we have to watch it closely.

243
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:13,600
Matthew?

244
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:16,080
I have two questions.

245
00:24:16,880 --> 00:24:37,200
So one is like Vincent, just on the physical eye side, how much do you worry about the labs?, the major labs, you know, as they continue to push forward and get closer and closer to AGI, obviously their AI becomes more generalized, which means it, and we're starting to see already with Astra that when you embody it, it's actually getting quite capable in a robotics site, which is quite, it was quite shocking for me to see its capabilities in those areas.

246
00:24:37,360 --> 00:24:46,000
Like, do you worry about the labs kind of coming in and muscling in and saying, we're just going to basically reach in to hardware and then we'll take the industrial worker?

247
00:24:46,160 --> 00:25:01,120
I mean, so what is the competitive advantage of a firm, a firm who's designing those hardware devices, but also let's say an industry which has a lot, how do they leverage that experience?ce and that data to get competitive advantage against a lab like OpenAI?

248
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:09,600
So we're seeing a lot of this in oil and gas where the robotic dogs are taking the place of humans for doing inspections.

249
00:25:10,400 --> 00:25:17,120
They can do pretty much anything the human can do, take pictures, scan, in hazardous areas where you don't want humans.

250
00:25:17,280 --> 00:25:18,480
So that's a very positive thing.

251
00:25:19,520 --> 00:25:26,400
You deposit that technology in a humanoid, put it in your home, that humanoid is walking your dog and cooking your meals.

252
00:25:27,760 --> 00:25:29,280
There are some safety concerns there.

253
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:31,440
It's on the horizon.

254
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:32,960
It's not in the twenty year horizon.

255
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:34,640
It looks like on the five year horizon.

256
00:25:35,040 --> 00:25:42,560
They'll be expensive at first, but I don't think there's anything holding back humanoids from entering our home in the near future.

257
00:25:44,880 --> 00:25:55,120
So I think we need to be very thoughtful about how that happens because the models essentially a robot is an embodied AI, an AI with a body.

258
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:59,280
And it's essentially taking these advanced models and putting it in the heads of robots.

259
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:15,040
And the fact that they're multimodal, that they understand video and audio native, makes them very effective in perceiving and managing the environment that these robots find themselves in.

260
00:26:15,840 --> 00:26:17,920
Vincent, I have one health question for you, if you don't mind.

261
00:26:18,400 --> 00:26:26,240
In the area of one thing I've been, it's been kind of shocking to me is how little development there has been around AI and around health, preventive health.

262
00:26:27,200 --> 00:26:39,760
We all have the risk, we have the watches and things that are monitoring our biometrics, but that data is predominantly going to big companies which basically produce Fitbits and things like that or Apple watches and things like that.

263
00:26:39,920 --> 00:26:44,800
And this drift towards centralization of one's personal medical data and things like that.

264
00:26:44,960 --> 00:26:47,200
The fact that there's very little sovereignty seems to be in that game.

265
00:26:47,360 --> 00:26:52,560
I'm not aware of a lot of companies that are trying to innovate, disrupt and provide more sovereignty and agency over one's personal data.

266
00:26:52,720 --> 00:26:54,960
It feels like it's still dominated by the big tech companies.

267
00:26:55,680 --> 00:26:58,000
Someone you used to work in kind of personal AI at HP.

268
00:26:58,240 --> 00:27:00,880
I'm just curious if you're seeing any signs of hope there.

269
00:27:02,720 --> 00:27:06,240
I think the hope is consistent with what you describe about local AI.

270
00:27:06,960 --> 00:27:18,640
So I think from a compute perspective and making sense of all your biometric data and then having some predictive outcomes, that's within compute's capabilities right now.

271
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:21,040
The issue is going to be where is that data stored?

272
00:27:22,560 --> 00:27:23,920
And I agree with you.

273
00:27:24,080 --> 00:27:28,960
I haven't seen and I'm tracking some of the healthcare stuff, and I haven't seen a lot of movement in that area.

274
00:27:30,480 --> 00:27:32,720
So if you're listening to this, there's a big opportunity there.

275
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:35,440
Please, God, step up.

276
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:46,000
Can I add with regards to healthcare, can I just add one area of focus that's very personal to me?

277
00:27:48,080 --> 00:27:57,120
which is, as you know, I left tech, big tech corporate to work in hospice and palliative.

278
00:27:57,440 --> 00:28:11,440
And one of the things that's occupies me is as AI gets better at diagnosis and almost everything in health care could be done better with AI.

279
00:28:11,760 --> 00:28:13,040
What is the last human role?

280
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:24,320
And for ontological, theological reasons, you may want a human chaplain, even though it's crankier and less present than the robot champlain is perfectly attentive.

281
00:28:25,920 --> 00:28:28,160
And that's something that occupies me.

282
00:28:28,320 --> 00:28:30,080
And it's a very good Catholic poem.

283
00:28:30,320 --> 00:28:31,360
You know what it reminds me of?

284
00:28:31,600 --> 00:28:39,600
Do you remember in the movie Alien, the original one, where the alien is on board and the captain of the ship goes and talks to the big computer and he's basically like, you know, what are our chances of making it out of here?

285
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:41,920
And the robot's like, you have no chance or something like that, right?

286
00:28:42,160 --> 00:28:44,480
And I remember that message, such a cold message, right?

287
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:46,080
Being like, they're abandoned all hope.

288
00:28:46,560 --> 00:28:48,720
Being delivered like in a screen as the characters are coming.

289
00:28:48,880 --> 00:28:49,760
You're just reading it, right?

290
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:50,960
How just, it just felt.

291
00:28:51,280 --> 00:28:54,240
While imagine, I thought, like, if that was a human being on the other end, right?

292
00:28:54,720 --> 00:28:57,120
You looked you in the eye and said, no, there is no hope.

293
00:28:57,360 --> 00:28:59,920
But there was just this, you're like, it's being delivered by another human being.

294
00:29:00,080 --> 00:29:01,840
You know that he's kind of sharing in your, in your fate.

295
00:29:01,920 --> 00:29:03,600
How that message would have just landed differently.

296
00:29:04,080 --> 00:29:05,840
And I think about that with hospice, right?

297
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:11,440
Just because I actually hope the doctor is using AI, because we're seeing now that they're better diagnosed than humans.

298
00:29:11,840 --> 00:29:20,960
At the same time, this is, I mean, I want that, you know, I won't be sitting there in my bed and all of a sudden the iPad next to me bings and I look over and, oh, apparently my chances have gone from ten to zero.

299
00:29:21,120 --> 00:29:24,400
You know, I like the idea that humans are not told you better go deliver that.

300
00:29:24,880 --> 00:29:28,880
This is why I am drawn to literature and literature.

301
00:29:28,880 --> 00:29:38,320
and cinema because they can convey that human accompaniment in situations where it just doesn't make sense.

302
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:41,840
So one of my most favorite movie scenes is from Heat.

303
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:42,720
Have you seen Heat?

304
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:43,200
Yeah, sure.

305
00:29:43,760 --> 00:29:53,440
So do you remember the last scene where Pacino's character shoots De Niro's character at the airport and De Niro's dying, bleeding out?

306
00:29:54,000 --> 00:30:00,160
And De Niro's character, who's the bank robber, holds out his hand and Pacino holds it.

307
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:01,680
Complete silence.

308
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:02,880
There were mortal enemies.

309
00:30:03,760 --> 00:30:05,920
That's a completely illogical.

310
00:30:06,240 --> 00:30:07,280
That's a powerful action.

311
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:08,320
It's so human.

312
00:30:09,760 --> 00:30:13,440
An algorithm and a robot, how do you program that?

313
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:15,520
So it's a mystery.

314
00:30:16,240 --> 00:30:23,360
Let's wrap up with a one sentence answer to what is Catholic AI?

315
00:30:25,040 --> 00:30:32,560
So I'll start with probably the most practiced answer to that question, and then we'll go down to Vincent.

316
00:30:33,680 --> 00:30:36,480
I think it's yet to be defined.

317
00:30:36,880 --> 00:30:50,240
But the way we understand it as a company is a Catholic AI is one that in its outputs as well as its internal considerations is always taking into account the matastereum and doing everything it possibly can to be as faithful as possible.

318
00:30:51,200 --> 00:30:51,520
Keller.

319
00:30:52,320 --> 00:30:59,920
I think it's a limit that can't be reached that helps us understand what it means to be a human person in the Catholic Anthropology.

320
00:31:01,280 --> 00:31:05,520
For me, Catholic AI is all about human flourishing and lifting up the person.

321
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:10,720
If we use AI correctly within our Catholic faith, we'll be better people.

322
00:31:11,280 --> 00:31:13,920
and we'll flourish in our world.

323
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:34,720
I think for me, similar answers, which is human dignity is at the heart or at the root of the approach to life in general, and technology is an amplifier, you know, of both our best and worst selves, and the Catholic tradition is capacious enough to reflect on that.

324
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:35,920
All right?

325
00:31:36,880 --> 00:31:37,600
Thanks, gentlemen.

326
00:31:38,320 --> 00:31:38,560
Thank you.

327
00:31:38,960 --> 00:31:40,320
And that's the Catholic Futurist.
